-- | ROUNDING DIRECTION: unreachable, not merely un-anchorable.
--
-- The earlier note here said the direction could not be anchored because Daml
-- `Decimal` is `Numeric 10` and no Daml literal can carry an eleventh
-- fractional digit. That is true, and it is the wrong reason: it makes a
-- deliberate property of the contract look like an accident of what one of the
-- four implementations can spell.
--
-- The real reason is that `amountUnits` is LOSSLESS-OR-REJECT in every
-- implementation, so no input of any kind reaches a rounding decision:
--
--   * Daml (`ArCCade.GameSdk.Digest.amountUnits`) truncates `d * 1e10` and then
--     requires `intToDecimal u / 1e10 == d`. On a `Numeric 10` the scaling is
--     exact — multiplying by 1e10 moves the last representable digit into the
--     ones place — so `truncate` never drops anything and the guard never
--     fires. Both halves are dead weight against a value that cannot exist.
--   * Java takes `setScale(0, RoundingMode.DOWN)` and then rejects unless the
--     value divides back exactly. Every input that could distinguish DOWN from
--     any other RoundingMode is refused by the very next statement, so the
--     rounding mode on that line is unobservable.
--   * JavaScript and Python never scale at all until they have checked that
--     every digit past the tenth is a zero, and throw if one is not.
--
-- So the direction is not an unpinned rule; it is a rule with no reachable
-- input, in all four implementations. What IS normative — and what a port can
-- actually get wrong — is the refusal itself, and that is pinned in the
-- conformance manifest as decision D12 with
-- `amount-negative-sub-unit-refused-not-rounded` carrying it. A client that
-- rounds instead of refusing turns that decision red.
--
-- What stays here is the pair of facts that make the claim above checkable
-- rather than assertable: the direction Daml's `truncate` actually has (which
-- is what `amountUnits` would use if it ever mattered), and the exactness of
-- the scaling at both ends of the representable band, which is what makes it
-- never matter.
roundingDirectionIsUnreachable : Script ()
roundingDirectionIsUnreachable = script do
  -- The direction, if it were ever reached: toward zero on both signs.
  truncate (1.9 : Decimal) === 1
  truncate (-1.9 : Decimal) === (-1)
  truncate (-0.9 : Decimal) === 0
  -- ...and the reason it is never reached: the round-trip the guard tests is
  -- an identity on every Decimal, checked at the finest unit and at both ends
  -- of the band, where an inexact scaling would show up first.
  intToDecimal (amountUnits 0.0000000001) / 1e10 === 0.0000000001
  intToDecimal (amountUnits (-0.0000000001)) / 1e10 === (-0.0000000001)
  intToDecimal (amountUnits 922337203.6854775807) / 1e10 === 922337203.6854775807
  intToDecimal (amountUnits (-922337203.6854775808)) / 1e10 === (-922337203.6854775808)
